## {{ CLI_NAME }} (output engine)

This project uses the `{{ CLI_NAME }}` CLI to keep AI-generated content maintainable.
Outputs live in `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/<name>/`. Always prefer
outputs over one-off generation.

### Before writing any new feature or file

1. Run `{{ CLI_NAME }} output search -q "<what you're about to do>"`.
   If a output matches (score > 0), run it instead:
   `{{ CLI_NAME }} output run <name> --var key=value ...`
   Preview first with `--dry-run` / `-d`, then run for real.
2. Only write fresh content if no output matches.

### After generating new files

If you just generated new files and the work seems repeatable, ask the user
whether they'd like to capture it as a output before moving on. Only do this
for new file generation — not for one-off edits.

If the user confirms, capture it:
```
{{ CLI_NAME }} output gen -n <short-name> \
  -i "<intent keywords>" \
  -v "var1,var2" \
  -o '{"files":[{"name":"...","template":"out.ts","outputPath":"..."}]}'
```
Then fill in the template at `{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/<name>/<template>`.
Prefer a `.ts` file with a default-export function `(vars) => string` — this is
the recommended format for new outputs. Nunjucks `.njk` templates
(`{{ '{{var}}' }}` syntax) are supported but should only be used when a `.ts`
template is impractical. Validate before considering it done:
`{{ CLI_NAME }} output validate -n <name>`

### `.ts` template format (recommended)

A `.ts` template is a TypeScript module that `export default`s a function.
At run time `{{ CLI_NAME }}` calls that function with the output's declared
variables (keyed by name, all strings) and writes the returned string to the
file's `outputPath`.

```ts
// ui-component/button.ts
export default ({ componentName, theme }: Record<string, string>) =>
  `<button class="${theme}">${componentName}</button>`;
```

- The default export must be a function `(vars: Record<string, string>) => string`.
  It receives every declared variable as a string and returns the full file
  contents as a string.
- Keep it a pure function of `vars` — do not import project code or read the
  filesystem. Compute the output string from `vars` alone.
- It runs natively on Bun/Deno; on Node it is executed in a child process with
  `--experimental-strip-types` (Node 22.6+), so write it as ESM with no
  side effects at module top level.
- If the module has no default function (or it isn't a function), `output run`
  fails with `Invalid .ts template: must export a default function that returns a
  string`. Note: `output validate` only checks that the template file exists —
  it does not import or execute it, so run the output once to confirm the
  default export works.

### Output schema (`{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/<name>/instructions.json`)

```json
{
  "name": "string",
  "variables": ["string"],
  "intent": ["string"],
  "output": {
    "files": [
      { "name": "string", "template": "string", "outputPath": "string" }
    ]
  },
  "includes": [
    {
      "name": "suboutput-name",
      "variables": { "subVar": "{{ '{{parentVar}}' }}", "other": "literal" },
      "files": { "originalName": "overridden/path" }
    }
  ]
}
```

`includes` is optional — see [Suboutputs](#suboutputs) below.

### Suboutputs

A suboutput is a regular output that another output includes via the
`includes` field. When a output runs, it renders its own files, then resolves
each included suboutput — mapping the suboutput's declared variables to values
from the parent (using `{{ '{{parentVar}}' }}` tokens or literals), optionally
overriding output paths, and rendering the suboutput's templates. Suboutputs
are just outputs — they live in their own folder under
`{{ MAIN_FOLDER }}/{{ OUTPUTS_FOLDER }}/`, have their own `instructions.json`
and templates, and can be run standalone or included by multiple parents.

#### Worked example

- *Before:* You have an `api-route` output that generates a route handler, a
types file, and a test. Later you build a `graphql-resolver` output that also
needs a types file with the same structure.
- *Extraction:* Create a standalone `types` output
  (`{{ CLI_NAME }} output gen -n types ...`). Add it to both parents'
  `instructions.json`:

```json
"includes": [
  {
    "name": "types",
    "variables": { "entityName": "{{ '{{modelName}}' }}" },
    "files": { "types": "src/types/{{ '{{entityName}}' }}.ts" }
  }
]
```

- The `variables` map says: "pass the parent's `modelName` as the suboutput's
  `entityName`." The `files` map says: "write the suboutput's `types` file to
  this path instead of its default."
- Validate: `{{ CLI_NAME }} output validate -n api-route`

#### When to extract suboutputs

Create outputs first. Extract suboutputs only when a concrete use case
demands it — you often don't know what should be a suboutput until you are
faced with the actual repetition. Extract when:

- You are duplicating the same template files across multiple outputs
- A output is generating files for two distinct concerns that change
  independently
- You find yourself copying a subset of one output's output into another

Do NOT preemptively decompose a output into suboutputs upfront. Build the
full output, verify it works, then extract when repetition emerges.

### Quick reference

| Action | Command |
|--------|---------|
| Search outputs | `{{ CLI_NAME }} output search -q "..."` |
| Run a output | `{{ CLI_NAME }} output run <name> --var k=v [-d]` |
| Create a output | `{{ CLI_NAME }} output gen -n <name> -i "..." -v "..." -o '...'` |
| Validate outputs | `{{ CLI_NAME }} output validate [-n <name>]` |
| Usage metrics | `{{ CLI_NAME }} metrics summary` |
